  "answer": "I'll look through the product special-price path and see why an end date keeps the storefront from showing the discount.The discount check looks inverted when both dates are set. I'll trace how those dates are saved and compared.The admin product mapper stores the special end date in the start-date field, and the price check then compares the start date against itself. I'll correct both.A special price that included an end date never reached the storefront as a discount. When a product was created through the admin API, `PersistableProductAvailabilityMapper` stored that end date in the start-date field, and `ProductPriceUtils` then checked the start date against itself, so the discount was skipped whenever both dates were present. The end date is now saved on the end-date field, and the price check uses the end date to decide whether the special is still active.\n\nVERIFICATION: {\"ran\": [\"mvn -pl sm-core -am test -Dtest=ProductPriceDiscountTest -DfailIfNoTests=false\"], \"result\": \"passed\", \"could_not_verify\": [\"storefront HTTP API and admin product create end to end\"], \"missing\": [\"a running Shopizer instance and an admin API fixture that creates a product with a special end date\"]}",
